---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'session', label: 'Start a session' },
   { id: 'move', label: 'Move and read' },
   { id: 'structure', label: 'Navigate by heading, link, or landmark' },
   { id: 'page', label: 'Read the whole page' },
   { id: 'check', label: 'Transcript assertions' },
   { id: 'batch', label: 'Run a batch of actions' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Run named screen reader commands' },
   { id: 'transcript', label: 'Checkpoints and transcripts' },
   { id: 'form', label: 'Example: a dialog' },
   { id: 'cleanup', label: 'Stop the session' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test with a screen reader"
   summary="Start a screen reader session, move through the page one element at a time, and check what the screen reader announced. The same commands drive VoiceOver, NVDA, and the simulated screen reader."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="session">
      <h2>Start a session</h2>
      <p>
         A session is a running screen reader that later <code>a1 sr</code> commands control.
         <code>sr start</code> takes the page URL as its argument and the screen reader as
         <code>--sr</code>: <code>voiceover</code>, <code>nvda</code>, or <code>virtual</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         For VoiceOver or NVDA, a11ied opens the URL in a browser, waits until that window is in
         front, and starts the screen reader. <code>--browser chrome</code>, <code>edge</code>,
         <code>brave</code>, <code>chromium</code>, or an app name such as <code>Safari</code>
         picks the browser. Without it, a11ied uses the system automation browser.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr start --sr voiceover --browser Safari http://localhost:3000/checkout</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>--app &lt;name&gt;</code> reads a native app that is already open instead of a
         page. The session brings the app to the front by name and waits for it to get there.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover --app Calendar</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>--sr virtual</code> starts the simulated screen reader: a screen reader implemented
         in software that runs inside a headless Chromium page and produces the same kind of
         announcements as VoiceOver or NVDA, with neither installed. It needs
         <code>--allow-virtual</code> when VoiceOver or NVDA is available, to confirm you want the
         simulation.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/dialog.html</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>Session ready

  Target:        virtual
  Engine:        browser (a headless Chromium page; the page scripts run)
  URL:           http://localhost:3000/dialog.html
  Recording:     none
  Idle timeout:  30 min

Every sr command now uses this session. Stop it with: a1 sr stop</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>Target:</code> is the screen reader that started. <code>Engine:</code> is
         <code>browser</code> when the page runs in headless Chromium, so a live region, a part
         of the page marked to announce its own content changes, is heard when a script fills
         it, and a dialog is heard when a click opens it. When no Chrome, Edge, Brave, or
         Chromium is installed, the engine is <code>jsdom</code>, an in-memory document where page
         scripts do not run, and only what is in the markup is announced. See
         <a href="/screen-readers#virtual">screen readers</a> for the details of both engines.
      </p>
      <p>
         One session is active at a time. Every later <code>sr</code> command uses it, and no
         command takes a session id. A second <code>sr start</code> stops the first session and
         reports it in the warnings. When the browser window never came to the front, the result
         has a <code>window-focus-unconfirmed</code> warning that lists the window in front
         instead.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="move">
      <h2>Move and read</h2>
      <p>
         A screen reader moves through a page one element at a time: a heading, a link, a button,
         a form field, a paragraph, a landmark. The element under its cursor is the current
         element. Each time the cursor moves, the screen reader announces the element it reached.
         The CLI prints that announcement as <code>Phrase:</code>, and the element's text as
         <code>Item:</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr next
a1 sr next heading</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr next  virtual
  Phrase:  main
  Item:    none
  Role:    main

sr next heading  virtual
  Phrase:  heading, Dialog demo, level 1
  Item:    Dialog demo
  Role:    heading level 1
  Name:    Dialog demo</code></pre>
      <p>
         The first command moved the cursor onto the <code>main</code> landmark, which has no
         text of its own. The second jumped to the next heading and printed its level and name.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr next</code>, <code>sr previous</code></div>
            <span>Move the cursor to the next or previous element and print the announcement.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr top</code>, <code>sr bottom</code></div>
            <span>Move the cursor to the first or last element of the document, or of the group the cursor is inside.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr interact</code>, <code>sr stop-interacting</code>, <code>sr activate</code></div>
            <span>Enter the group or control under the cursor, leave it, or press the element under the cursor as a click would.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr escape</code>
            <span>Press Escape, which closes a menu or a dialog.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr read</code>
            <span>Print the current element without moving: role, name, value, states, the announcement, and which of those came from the page and which from the announcement.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr title</code>
            <span>Print the page title: <code>document.title</code> on the simulated screen reader, the window summary (VO-F2) on VoiceOver, the window title (NVDA-T) on NVDA.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr find "Learn more"</code>
            <span>Move the cursor to the next element whose text contains the argument. Exits 4 when no element does. Needs an active session.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr press Tab Tab</code>
            <span>Press key chords in order, one chord per argument.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr type "test@example.com"</code>
            <span>Type into the focused form field.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr open &lt;url&gt;</code>
            <span>Load another page in the running session.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 sr read</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr read  virtual
  Phrase:  heading, Dialog demo, level 1
  Item:    Dialog demo
  Role:    heading level 1
  Name:    Dialog demo
  Source:  virtual: role and name from the active node (dom-accessibility-api), states from its attributes</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>Source:</code> says where the role, name, and states came from. The simulated
         screen reader reads them from the DOM node under its cursor. VoiceOver reports the
         element's text and an announcement in the form <code>name, role</code>, so the role and
         states are parsed from the announcement and the name is the element's text. NVDA reports
         only the announcement, so all three are parsed from it.
      </p>
      <p>
         With <code>--json</code>, every response has a <code>state</code> object with
         <code>lastSpokenPhrase</code> (the announcement), <code>currentItemText</code> (the
         element's text), <code>currentItem</code> (the role, name, value, states, and heading
         level, plus a <code>source</code> field), the running <code>spokenPhraseLog</code> (every
         announcement so far), and the timestamped <code>transcript</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Add <code>--phrase</code> to print the announcement alone, one line, for shell loops. Add
         <code>--timeout &lt;ms&gt;</code> to bound how long the screen reader command may take.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="structure">
      <h2>Navigate by heading, link, or landmark</h2>
      <p>
         Screen reader users do not read pages one element at a time. They jump by heading,
         link, or landmark: a section of the page marked with a role such as
         <code>main</code>, <code>navigation</code>, or <code>banner</code>, so a screen reader
         can skip straight to it. <code>sr next &lt;kind&gt;</code> and
         <code>sr previous &lt;kind&gt;</code> do the same on every screen reader. The kinds are
         <code>heading</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>landmark</code>, <code>control</code>,
         <code>button</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>graphic</code>,
         <code>region</code>, and <code>form-field</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr next heading
a1 sr next heading --level 2
a1 sr previous link
a1 sr next control --times 3</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>sr elements &lt;kind&gt;</code> works like the VoiceOver rotor or the NVDA elements
         list: it moves the cursor to the top of the page and prints every element of one kind
         with its announcement. It stops at the end of the page or at <code>--max</code>, which
         defaults to 200, and says which. <code>elements</code>, <code>read-all</code>,
         <code>goto</code>, and <code>find</code> need an active session and exit with
         <code>missing-session</code> without one. Run
         <code>sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual &lt;url&gt;</code> first for the simulated
         screen reader, since a plain <code>sr start</code> starts VoiceOver on macOS.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr elements heading  virtual
4 heading elements from the top of the page.

1. heading level 1: Structure page  heading, Structure page, level 1
2. heading level 2: Products  heading, Products, level 2
3. heading level 3: Pricing  heading, Pricing, level 3
4. heading level 2: Sign up  heading, Sign up, level 2

Stopped at the end of the document.</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each kind maps to Guidepup's jump method or a VoiceOver Commander command, to an NVDA
         single-letter key, and to a <code>virtual.commands.moveToNext*</code> command. The
         simulated screen reader has no command for button, control, table, list, graphic, and
         form-field, so it moves element by element until the announced role matches, and reports
         <code>moved: false</code> when the page has none.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr goto --role button --name "Open dialog"</code> moves the cursor forward until the
         current element has that role, that name, or both, and exits 4 when nothing matches
         before the end of the page.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr goto --role button --name Open dialog  virtual
  Phrase:  button, Open dialog
  Item:    Open dialog
  Role:    button
  Name:    Open dialog
  Found:   yes</code></pre>
      <p>
         Inside a table, <code>sr table next-cell</code>, <code>previous-cell</code>,
         <code>next-row</code>, <code>previous-row</code>, <code>next-column</code>, and
         <code>previous-column</code> move between cells. <code>sr table column-header</code> and
         <code>row-header</code> print the headers of the current cell. VoiceOver moves with
         VO-arrows inside an interacted table and reads the column header with VO-C. NVDA moves
         with Control-Alt-arrows and speaks headers as the cursor enters a cell, so the two header
         commands exit 2 on NVDA with that explanation.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="page">
      <h2>Read the whole page</h2>
      <p>
         Two commands read a page. <code>sr walk</code> reads a whole one and sets up the
         session for you. <code>sr read-all</code> reads from wherever the cursor already is,
         in a session you already started.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr read-all</code> moves the cursor element by element from its current position to
         the end of the document, bounded by <code>--max</code> (default 500), and prints the
         transcript: every announcement in order, with a timestamp each. It says whether it stopped
         at the end or at the cap.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr walk [url]</code> does all of this in one command: it starts a session when
         none is active, or loads the URL in the active one, moves the cursor to the top, runs
         <code>read-all</code>, and prints the transcript. With no session and no
         <code>--sr</code>, <code>walk</code> starts VoiceOver on macOS. Pass
         <code>--sr virtual --allow-virtual</code> for the simulated screen reader instead.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/dialog.html --sr virtual --allow-virtual
a1 sr walk --max 40 --out ./transcripts/checkout-walk.md</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>! No session was active, so one was started.
# Transcript: virtual on http://localhost:3000/dialog.html

Started 2026-09-06T11:07:17.840Z. Exported 2026-09-06T11:07:18.044Z. 5 phrases.

1. [11:07:18.043] main
2. [11:07:18.043] heading, Dialog demo, level 1 (Dialog demo)
3. [11:07:18.043] button, Open dialog (Open dialog)
4. [11:07:18.043] end of main
5. [11:07:18.043] end of document

Stopped at the end of the document.</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each numbered line is one element the cursor stopped at and what the screen reader said
         there. The text in parentheses is the element's text, when it has any.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="check">
      <div class="stack">
         <h2>Transcript assertions</h2>
         <p>
            <code>sr expect</code> searches the transcript for an announcement and exits 4 when
            none matches. Plain text matches without regard to case.
            <code>/pattern/flags</code> is a regular expression. <code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code>
            searches only the announcements after that checkpoint, and <code>--not</code> passes
            when nothing matches.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr expect "dialog, Preferences" --since opened
a1 sr expect /error/i --not</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr expect
  Expected:  "dialog, Preferences"
  Checked:   2 phrases since "opened"
  Matched:   dialog, Preferences, modal
  Result:    pass</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>sr wait --for &lt;text|/regex/&gt;</code> polls the transcript until an
            announcement made after the wait began matches, and exits 4 at
            <code>--timeout</code> (default 5000 ms). It reads the whole transcript rather than
            the last announcement, so an announcement that arrives and is replaced before the
            next poll still counts.
         </p>
         <p>
            Use it for an announcement that arrives later than the command that caused it, such
            as a live region a network response fills, or the delay before VoiceOver finishes
            speaking. An announcement the triggering command already printed in its own output
            happened before the wait began, so run <code>expect</code> for that one instead.
            Without <code>--for</code>, <code>wait</code> pauses for <code>--ms</code>.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr press Enter
a1 sr wait --for /saved/i --timeout 3000</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Exit codes</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>find</code>, <code>goto</code>, <code>wait --for</code>, <code>expect</code>, and a
            <code>batch</code> with a failed expect exit 4. Usage errors, including a command the
            screen reader cannot run or a command run with no active session, exit 2. A browser
            or broker process that fails to start exits 3.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="batch">
      <h2>Run a batch of actions</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr batch [file]</code> reads one action per line, from the file or from stdin, and
         runs them all over one connection to the session process. Each line is the JSON request
         the MCP <code>sr_action</code> tool takes, or an <code>expect</code> line. Blank lines and
         lines starting with <code>#</code> are skipped. The batch stops at the first failed expect
         unless <code>--continue</code> is set.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr batch &lt;&lt;'EOF'
&#123;"action":"goto","payload":&#123;"role":"button","name":"Open dialog"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"checkpoint","payload":&#123;"label":"opened"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"activate"&#125;
&#123;"action":"expect","payload":&#123;"match":"Preferences","since":"opened"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"escape"&#125;
&#123;"action":"expect","payload":&#123;"match":"Open dialog"&#125;&#125;
EOF</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr batch
✓ 1. goto &#123;"role":"button","name":"Open dialog","max":500&#125;  button, Open dialog
✓ 2. checkpoint &#123;"label":"opened"&#125;  button, Open dialog
✓ 3. activate  textbox, Nickname
✓ 4. expect Preferences since "opened"  pass
✓ 5. escape  button, Open dialog
✓ 6. expect Open dialog  pass

6 steps passed.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The checkpoint runs before <code>activate</code>, not after, so <code>expect --since</code>
         searches the dialog's announcement. An announcement already printed by the triggering
         action happened before a later checkpoint and would not count.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="commands">
      <h2>Run named screen reader commands</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr do</code> runs a command by name. The portable set works on every screen reader
         and resolves through the same table the subcommands use: <code>next</code>,
         <code>previous</code>, <code>interact</code>, <code>stop-interacting</code>,
         <code>activate</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>escape</code>, and
         <code>next-&lt;kind&gt;</code> and <code>previous-&lt;kind&gt;</code> for every kind above.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr do interact --json
a1 sr do next-heading --json
a1 sr do stop-interacting --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         VoiceOver and NVDA add their own sets. <code>sr list</code> prints them grouped by command
         set and by what the commands do. Start with <code>--query</code>: the full list is over
         400 lines.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr list --query heading
a1 sr list --query "VO-Command" --sr voiceover</code></pre>
      <p>
         When two sets share a name, prefix it with the set:
         <code>sr do voiceover-keycode:move-to-next</code> or
         <code>sr do voiceover-commander:move-right</code>. <code>--command-set</code> picks one
         set for the whole call. The simulated screen reader accepts the portable set only.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="transcript">
      <h2>Checkpoints and transcripts</h2>
      <p>
         A checkpoint is a named marker in the transcript. <code>sr transcript --since</code> and
         <code>sr expect --since</code> then read only the announcements after it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr checkpoint "opened the dialog"
a1 sr next
a1 sr transcript --since "opened the dialog"</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each transcript entry has an index, an ISO timestamp, the announcement, and the
         element's text when the screen reader reported one. The Markdown output numbers the
         announcements 1, 2, 3 and prints a checkpoint as a heading between them.
         <code>--tail &lt;count&gt;</code> keeps the last N announcements.
         <code>--out &lt;path&gt;</code> writes the transcript to a file, as JSON for a
         <code>.json</code> path and as Markdown for a <code>.md</code> path. Pass
         <code>--format json</code> or <code>--format md</code> to pick the format yourself.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr transcript --out ./transcripts/checkout.md</code></pre>
      <p>
         On VoiceOver, <code>sr screenshot &lt;path&gt;</code> saves a picture of the element the
         cursor is on. NVDA has no cursor screenshot and the simulated screen reader has no screen,
         so both exit 2 and say so.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="form">
      <h2>Example: a dialog</h2>
      <p>
         The page has one heading and a button named Open dialog. The button opens a modal dialog
         named Preferences and moves focus to its first field. This transcript comes from
         <code>sr start</code>, <code>sr next</code>, <code>sr next heading</code>,
         <code>sr goto --role button --name "Open dialog"</code>,
         <code>sr checkpoint opened</code>, and <code>sr activate</code>, in that order, followed
         by <code>sr transcript</code>.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code># Transcript: virtual on http://localhost:3000/dialog.html

Started 2026-09-06T11:07:38.042Z. Exported 2026-09-06T11:07:44.590Z. 6 phrases.

1. [11:07:38.042] document
2. [11:07:38.876] main
3. [11:07:39.749] heading, Dialog demo, level 1 (Dialog demo)
4. [11:07:41.203] button, Open dialog (Open dialog)

## opened (11:07:41.880)

5. [11:07:42.555] dialog, Preferences, modal (Preferences)
6. [11:07:42.555] textbox, Nickname (Nickname)</code></pre>
      <p>
         Lines 1 to 4 are the elements the cursor stopped at on the way to the button. The
         <code>opened</code> heading is the checkpoint, placed before <code>activate</code>. Lines
         5 and 6 are what the screen reader announced when the button was pressed: the dialog,
         with its name and its <code>modal</code> state, and then the text field that received
         focus. Three things to check in a transcript like this. Every control has a name, so a
         line that reads <code>button</code> alone is a defect. The order matches the visual
         order. The states that matter are announced, which is why <code>modal</code> appears on
         line 5.
      </p>
      <p>
         For a validation message, submit the form with <code>sr press Enter</code>, then run
         <code>sr wait --for /error/i</code> to find out whether the message was announced.
         Status Messages is criterion 4.1.3, and its <a href="/test-methods">test method</a> is
         hybrid because the announcement is the part a person has to judge.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="cleanup">
      <h2>Stop the session</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop --out ./transcripts/checkout.json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Stopping quits the screen reader process, finishes any recording, and deletes the
         session state file. A session left running keeps VoiceOver or NVDA under automation
         control, so stop each one you start. A session nobody has used for 30 minutes stops
         itself. Pass <code>--idle-timeout &lt;minutes&gt;</code> on <code>sr start</code> to
         change that, or <code>--idle-timeout 0</code> to turn it off.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr stop --out</code> writes the transcript on the way out. When the session recorded
         video, a11ied also writes the Markdown transcript next to the recording file, so you can
         read the two together.
      </p>
      <p>
         For a single action with no session, add <code>--ephemeral</code> to
         <code>press</code>, <code>type</code>, or <code>do</code>. It starts a session, runs the
         action, and stops the session in one command.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
